 abstract	= {A steady state model of the chemistry in the lower troposphere is developed in which the number densities of major atmospheric species, N$_2$, O$_2$, H$_2$O, CH$_4$, CO, O$_3$, N$_2$O, NH$_3$ and NO $+$ NO$_2$ are held constant at values appropriate to a summer latitude of 34$^\circ$. Hourly daytime concentrations are then calculated for HO$_2$, OH, CH$_3$O$_2$, CH$_3$O, H$_2$C=O, NO, NO$_2$, NO$_3$, N$_2$O$_5$, HNO$_3$ and HNO$_2$ along with a representative evening value.

The major products of the nitrogen daytime chemistry are HNO$_3$ and HNO$_2$. The apparent controlling loss mechanism for HNO$_3$, heterogeneous reaction of HNO$_3$ on particulate surfaces leading to the formation of inorganic nitrates, is also an important mechanism for removing oxides of nitrogen from the air. The calculated rate of removal greatly exceeds the estimates of Erickson (1952) though some critical rate coefficients are in doubt and the effect of bacterial action has not been considered.

The dominant radicals, HO$_2$ and CH$_3$O$_2$, have daytime concentrations approaching $1.2 \times 10^9$ molecules~cm$^{-3}$ and $5 \times 10^8$ molecules~cm$^{-3}$ respectively. The concentration of OH approaches $1.7 \times 10^7$ molecules~cm$^{-3}$. At night HO$_2$ and CH$_3$O$_2$ are the only radical species present in a significant amount. Chain reactions which interconvert HO$_2$ and OH also serve as a source of H$_2$C=O and a sink for CO, H$_2$ and CH$_4$. It is of interest that the CH$_4$ sink ultimately results in the production of CO and H$_2$ thus providing a large natural source for both. The concentrations of H$_2$C=O, H$_2$O$_2$ and CH$_2$OOH are all calculated to be in the \textit{ppb} range.}
}

